\description{
\code{blvim()} computes equilibrium flows between origin and destination locations
in the Harris and Wilson spatial interaction model. At equilibrium,
destination attractivenesses satisfy
\deqn{
  \forall j,\quad
  \kappa_j Z_j = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Y_{ij},
}
where \eqn{Y_{ij}} is the flow from origin \eqn{i} to destination \eqn{j},
\eqn{Z_j} is the attractiveness of destination \eqn{j}, and
\eqn{\kappa_j} converts attractiveness into incoming flow. Flows are
computed using the production-constrained entropy-maximising model
implemented by \code{\link[=static_blvim]{static_blvim()}}.
}
\details{
Several iterative algorithms are available. The model can be run from a
single initial attractiveness vector or from several starting points. In
the latter case, the solution with the largest final potential is returned.
\subsection{Initialisation and multiple runs}{

Initial destination attractivenesses are specified through \code{Z}:
\itemize{
\item If \code{Z} is \code{NULL}, one starting point is constructed automatically.
\item If \code{Z} is a numeric vector, the algorithm is run once from that vector.
\item If \code{Z} is a numeric matrix, each row is used as an independent starting
point.
\item If \code{Z} is an object created by \code{\link[=blvim_initialise]{blvim_initialise()}}, its initialiser is
evaluated using the model context. The resulting vector or matrix is then
handled in the same way as an explicitly supplied \code{Z}.
}

In the bipartite case, automatic initialisation uses the centre of mass of
the weighted simplex:

\deqn{
  Z_j^0 =
  \frac{\sum_i X_i}{p\kappa_j},
}

where \eqn{p} is the number of destinations. In the non-bipartite case, it
uses

\deqn{
  Z_j^0 = \frac{X_j}{\kappa_j}.
}

Automatic initialisation therefore produces a single run. Use a matrix or
\code{\link[=blvim_initialise]{blvim_initialise()}} to perform multiple runs.

For multiple runs, all starting points use the same model parameters,
algorithm, convergence settings, and algorithm-specific controls. The final
potential is evaluated for each run, and the run with the strictly largest
potential is selected. If several runs have the same largest potential, the
first is selected.

Summaries of all runs are stored in the returned model and can be obtained
with \code{\link[=sim_logs]{sim_logs()}}. If \code{keep_runs = TRUE}, the complete fitted models are
retained and can be obtained with \code{\link[=sim_runs]{sim_runs()}}.
}

\subsection{Boltzmann-Lotka-Volterra algorithms}{

At each iteration of a Boltzmann-Lotka-Volterra (BLV) algorithm, flows are
computed with \code{\link[=static_blvim]{static_blvim()}}. The total flow received by destination
\eqn{j} is

\deqn{
  D_j = \sum_{i=1}^{n} Y_{ij}.
}

Attractivenesses are then updated to bring \eqn{\kappa_j Z_j} closer to
\eqn{D_j}. Two BLV algorithms are available:
\enumerate{
\item \code{"linear"} uses the original update rule proposed by Harris and Wilson
(1978):

\deqn{
     Z_j^{t+1}
     =
     Z_j^t
     +
     \epsilon
     \left(
       \frac{D_j^t}{\kappa_j} - Z_j^t
     \right).
   }
\item \code{"quadratic"} uses the update rule proposed by Wilson (2008):

\deqn{
     Z_j^{t+1}
     =
     Z_j^t
     +
     \epsilon
     \left(
       \frac{D_j^t}{\kappa_j} - Z_j^t
     \right) Z_j^t.
   }
}

For both algorithms, \eqn{\epsilon} is specified by \code{epsilon} and should
generally be smaller than one. Convergence is checked every \code{conv_check}
iterations and is attained when

\deqn{
  \lVert Z^{t+1} - Z^t \rVert
  <
  \delta
  \left(
    \lVert Z^{t+1} \rVert + \delta
  \right),
}

where \eqn{\delta} is specified by \code{precision}. Iteration stops at
convergence or after \code{iter_max} iterations.
}

\subsection{Gradient algorithms}{

Computing an equilibrium is equivalent to maximising the potential
function; see \code{\link[=sim_potential]{sim_potential()}}. The gradient algorithms optimise this
potential directly using projected gradient ascent.

The \code{"gradient"} algorithm works directly with the attractivenesses
\eqn{Z_j}. The \code{"gradient_unit"} algorithm works with rescaled variables
\eqn{U_j} satisfying

\deqn{
  \sum_{j=1}^{p} U_j = 1.
}

The rescaling can accelerate convergence in some settings. The two
algorithms optimise equivalent formulations and should produce the same
solution up to numerical precision. They can nevertheless converge to
solutions different from those obtained with the \code{"linear"} and
\code{"quadratic"} algorithms.

Both gradient algorithms use projected ascent with a backtracking line
search and an Armijo sufficient-increase condition. Convergence is assessed
using a duality gap with tolerance \code{precision}, and the number of iterations
is limited by \code{iter_max}. The \code{epsilon} and \code{conv_check} arguments are not
used. Line-search and other internal parameters can be modified through
\code{control}; see \code{\link[=blvim_gradient_control]{blvim_gradient_control()}}.
}
}
\section{Location data}{
While models in this package do not use location data beyond \code{X} and \code{Z},
additional data can be stored and used when analysing spatial interaction
models.
\subsection{Origin and destination location names}{

Spatial interaction models can store names for origin and destination
locations, using \code{\link[=origin_names<-]{origin_names<-()}} and \code{\link[=destination_names<-]{destination_names<-()}}. Names
are taken by default from names of the cost matrix \code{costs}. More precisely,
\code{rownames(costs)} is used for origin location names and \code{colnames(costs)} for
destination location names.
}

\subsection{Origin and destination location positions}{

Spatial interaction models can store the positions of the origin and
destination locations, using \code{\link[=origin_positions<-]{origin_positions<-()}} and
\code{\link[=destination_positions<-]{destination_positions<-()}}.
}

\subsection{Specifying location data}{

In addition to the functions mentioned above, location data can be specified
directly using the \code{origin_data} and \code{destination_data} parameters. Data are
given by a list whose components are not interpreted excepted the following
ones:
\itemize{
\item \code{names} is used to specify location names and its content has to follow
the restrictions documented in \code{\link[=origin_names<-]{origin_names<-()}} and
\code{\link[=destination_names<-]{destination_names<-()}}
\item \code{positions} is used to specify location positions and its content has
to follow the restrictions documented in \code{\link[=origin_positions<-]{origin_positions<-()}} and
\code{\link[=destination_positions<-]{destination_positions<-()}}
}
}
}

\examples{
distances <- french_cities_distances[1:10, 1:10] / 1000
production <- rep(1, 10)
attractiveness <- log(french_cities$area[1:10])

# Rescale attractivenesses to total production
attractiveness <- attractiveness / sum(attractiveness) * sum(production)

# Original Harris-Wilson update rule
linear_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250, attractiveness,
  algorithm = "linear"
)
linear_flows

# Wilson's quadratic update rule
quadratic_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250, attractiveness,
  algorithm = "quadratic"
)

# Gradient ascent on the potential
gradient_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250, attractiveness,
  algorithm = "gradient"
)

# Gradient ascent using unit-simplex variables
unit_gradient_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250, attractiveness,
  algorithm = "gradient_unit"
)

# Automatic single-point initialisation
automatic_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250,
  algorithm = "gradient"
)

# Multiple explicitly supplied starting points
starts <- rbind(
  attractiveness,
  c(rep(0, 5), rep(sum(production) / 5, 5))
)
multistart_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250,
  Z = starts,
  algorithm = "gradient",
  keep_runs = TRUE
)

# Generate starting points when the model context is known
generated_flows <- blvim(
  distances, production, 1.5, 1 / 250,
  Z = blvim_initialise(n = 20, face_fraction = 0.5),
  algorithm = "gradient"
)

}
